<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shantarakshita</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Śāntarakṣita</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shantarakshita was an Indian Buddhist philosopher and abbot of the great monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a> who was invited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a> in the eighth century. He helped found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samye">Samye</a>, the first Buddhist monastery in Tibet, around 779, and ordained the first Tibetan monks. His philosophical work combined the Madhyamaka and Yogacara schools.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shantarakshita.jpg" alt="Shantarakshita" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suraj Belbase · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shanta_Rakshita_Khenchen_Bodhisattva_at_Guru_Lhakhang_Monastery_at_Bouddhanath.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Śāntarakṣita, whose name means protected by the One who is at peace, lived from 725 to 788 according to the dates given for him. Few historical records of him exist, and most of the available material is hagiographic. According to the 19th-century commentator Ju Mipham, he was the son of the king of Zahor, which some sources place around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagalpur">Bhagalpur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangka">Banka</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. He was an Indian monk of the Mulasarvastivada lineage who lived under the Pala Empire. Tibetan sources say he studied under Jñānagarbha at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a> and became its abbot after mastering all branches of learning. Tibetan sources call him, Jñānagarbha and Kamalaśīla the three eastern Svātantrikas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a> invited him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> to help establish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and a first journey can be dated to 763. According to Tibetan sources such as the Blue Annals, that visit failed because of the activities of local spirits, and he spent six years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> before returning. He came back with the tantric adept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmasambhava">Padmasambhava</a>, who performed the rites that appeased the spirits, and he then oversaw the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samye">Samye</a>, modelled on the Indian monastery of Uddaṇḍapura.</p><p>He ordained the first seven Tibetan monastics there, with the help of twelve Indian monks, about 779, and stayed as abbot for the rest of his life. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samye">Samye</a> he set up a monastic curriculum on the Indian model and oversaw the translation of scriptures into Tibetan, with scholars such as Vimalamitra, Buddhaguhya, Śāntigarbha and Viśuddhasiṃha. He and his students first taught the ten good actions, the six paramitas and dependent origination. Tibetan sources say he died suddenly after being kicked by a horse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Around eleven works may be his. His Tattvasaṅgraha is a compendium of more than 3,600 verses in 26 chapters that sets out and refutes the non-Buddhist views of his time, including Sāṅkhya, Nyāya and the Cārvākas. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> copy, with the commentary of his pupil Kamalaśīla, was found in 1873 by Dr G. Bühler in a Jain temple at Jaisalmer. His Madhyamakālaṅkāra, the Ornament of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-way">Middle Way</a>, sets out his synthesis of Madhyamaka with Yogācāra and the epistemology of Dharmakirti. It argues for emptiness by showing that things have neither a single nor a manifold nature, and it accepts mind-only teaching on the conventional level.</p><p>His approach stayed the main Tibetan reading of Madhyamaka until the 11th and 12th centuries, when Candrakirti's work began to be translated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsongkhapa">Tsongkhapa</a> later criticised it, and Prasaṅgika Madhyamaka replaced it as the dominant view. In the 19th century Ju Mipham revived it as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rime-movement">Rimé movement</a>, and the Madhyamakālaṅkāra is now studied by all Nyingma shedra students.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Śāntarakṣita” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trisong Detsen invited the Indian scholar <b>Shantarakshita</b> and, by tradition, the tantric master Padmasambhava, founded Samye monastery around 779 and, according to later accounts, presided over a debate around 792–794 in which Indian Buddhism prevailed over Chinese Chan teaching.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rewalsar, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.63,76.83&amp;year=764" aria-label="Rewalsar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6531 1012 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shantarakshita"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6981 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6981 1282h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.63,76.83&amp;year=764">What was here in 764 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
